{:edb/db-before-t 1138 :edb/db-after-t 1139 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534451 50 #inst "2026-10-11T20:57:47.456Z" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133170 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133175 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133176 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133177 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133182 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133187 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133660 1071 "A coin is biased so that the probability of it landing on heads is $0.4$. The coin is tossed $7$ times. What is the mean number of times the coin lands on heads?" 13194139534451 17592186045417 false] [17592186133660 1071 "A coin is biased so that the probability of it landing on heads is $0.4.$ The coin is tossed $7$ times. What is the mean number of times the coin lands on heads?" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133660 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin lands on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=7$ tosses\n- the probability of landing on heads for each toss is $p=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(7,0.4)$.\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X$, the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = np\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean of the number of times the coin lands on heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= np \\\\\n&= (7)(0.4) \\\\\n&= 2.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 false] [17592186133660 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin lands on heads. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=7$ tosses\n\n- the probability of landing on heads for each toss is $p=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(7,0.4).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean of the number of times the coin lands on heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(7)(0.4) \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133661 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ shots\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye for each arrow is $p=50\\%=0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.5).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of arrows hitting the bull's eye is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(12)(0.5)(1-0.5) \\\\  & =(6)(0.5) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133661 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ shots\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye for each arrow is $p=50\\%=0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.5)$.\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X$, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of arrows hitting the bull's eye is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= np(1 - p) \\\\\n&= (12)(0.5)(1 - 0.5) \\\\\n&= (6)(0.5) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 false] [17592186133662 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls of the die\n\n- the probability of getting an even number (i.e., a $2,$ or $4$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{2}{5} .\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B\\left(8,\\dfrac25\\right).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.92} \\\\  & =1.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133662 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls of the die\n- the probability of getting an even number (i.e., a $2$, or $4$) on any roll of the die is\n$p=\\dfrac{2}{5}$.\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left(8,\\dfrac25\\right)\n$$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X$, the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{np(1 - p)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8 \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot (1 - \\frac{2}{5})} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1.92} \\\\\n&= 1.386\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534451 17592186045417 false] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421983 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421985 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421987 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421984 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421986 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421988 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421991 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421989 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186133847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421990 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134018 1050 420.0 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134522 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins in a given season. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=60%=0.6$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.6).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean of the number of games won is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(12)(0.6) \\\\  & =7.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134523 1071 "A fair six-sided die is rolled $7$ times. What is the mean of the number of times that the outcome of a single roll is a $2$ or a $3$?" 13194139534451 17592186045417 false] [17592186134523 1071 "A fair six-sided die is rolled $7$ times. What is the mean of the number of times that the outcome of a single roll is a $2$ or a $3?$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134523 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a $2$ or $3$ is rolled.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=7$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a $2$ or $3$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of times we get a $2$ or a $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186134524 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins in a given season. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=30%=0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.3).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of games won is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(20)(0.3)(1-0.3) \\\\  & =(6)(0.7) \\\\  & =4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186135091 1064 "Remember that if $X$ is a binomial random variable in which there are $n$ experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the expected value, variance, and standard deviation of a binomial random variable $X$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= np \\\\\nVar[X] &= np(1 - p) \\\\\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{np(1 - p)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 false] [17592186135091 1064 "Remember that if $X$ is a binomial random variable in which there are $n$ experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the expected value, variance, and standard deviation of a binomial random variable $X$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\ \\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421929 1080 "\\frac{11}{4}" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421929 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421929 1083 #uuid "06c51642-996d-5135-870c-db34a80b53f0" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421930 1080 "\\frac{15}{2}" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421930 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421930 1083 #uuid "07cebf32-de57-5ac9-b739-e5dd0c9c0388" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421931 1080 "\\frac{36}{5}" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421931 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421931 1083 #uuid "0a1da0bd-fbca-576c-9d98-2c5457624d78" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421932 1080 "9" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421932 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421932 1083 #uuid "0e4bdfc2-c0e9-5af4-abe8-067d3285da99" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421933 1080 "4" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421933 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421933 1083 #uuid "12e44cce-8bca-5ac8-80a0-02abd7b3acd2" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421934 1080 "5.250" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421934 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421934 1083 #uuid "155de04a-19c5-548a-9a38-8b17697942c4" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421935 1080 "13" 13194139534451 17592186045417 true] [17592186421935 1082 #edb/ref 